Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; Victorian London; cold blues and candle gold; fog, frost, and firelight

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Fog hangs thick over the crowded paths. A sign reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, thin, pointed nose, blue thin lips, frosty rime on brows and wiry chin, black great-coat, clutches a ledger and edges through the crowd. People part. A blind man's dog tugs its owner into a doorway.

(SOUND: distant church bell, wheezing footsteps, foghorn wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A dismal office. Scrooge's small fire barely glows. Beyond, in a tank-like cell, BOB CRATCHIT, thin, threadbare, white comforter, copies letters at a desk with one coal of fire. Fog seeps at the keyhole. Candles flare like ruddy smears.

(SOUND: pen scratch, coal settle, clock tick)

The door bursts. FRED, young, ruddy, handsome, eyes sparkling, breath smoking, strides in glowing from the frost.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

FRED
I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; and I say, God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds, then pokes his tiny fire and extinguishes the last spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle! And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without anger. At the outer door he greets Bob warmly. Bob returns it.

SCROOGE
(muttering)
My clerk, with fifteen shillings a week, and a wife and family, talking about a merry Christmas. I'll retire to Bedlam.

Two portly CHARITY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Are there no prisons?

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
They are. Still.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his labours, pleased with himself.

(SOUND: door shut hard, fog wind at the chinks)

EXT. COUNTING-HOUSE DOOR - LATER

Dusk. A thin CAROL BOY stoops at the keyhole.

CAROL BOY
(singing)
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

Scrooge seizes a ruler. The boy flees into the fog.

(SOUND: ruler clatter, boy’s footsteps fleeing, frost crackle)

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - EVENING

Shutting time. Scrooge buttons his great-coat. Bob snuffs his candle and puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises. Scrooge growls out. Bob, long ends of white comforter dangling, locks up.

EXT. CORNHILL - NIGHT

Bob joins a lane of boys and goes down a slide twenty times, laughing, then runs hard toward Camden Town.

(SOUND: ice scrape, boys’ shouts, boots pelting)

INT. TAVERN - NIGHT

Scrooge takes a melancholy dinner alone, reads newspapers, studies his banker's-book.

(SOUND: cutlery on plate, low tavern murmur)

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS YARD - NIGHT

A gloomy pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang on the black gateway. Scrooge gropes to the door, key in hand. The large knocker becomes MARLEY'S FACE: motionless wide eyes, spectacles on ghostly forehead, hair stirred as by hot air, livid colour.

(SOUND: wind moan, distant chain hint)

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again. He turns the key sturdily, lights his candle.

INT. SCROOGE'S HALL AND STAIRS - NIGHT

He looks behind the door—only screws and nuts.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He bangs it shut. The sound thunders through the house. He double-checks, walks up the wide dark stairs trimming his candle, half-seeing a locomotive hearse in the gloom.

(SOUND: door boom and echoes, stair creak, candle sputter)

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

Sitting-room, bedroom beyond. Small fire, spoon and basin, little saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge, dressing-gown, slippers, nightcap, locks and double-locks the door, sits to his gruel. Dutch tiles around the old fireplace. Marley's face seems on every tile.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He paces, sits. A disused bell begins to swing—softly, then loudly. Every bell in the house answers.

(SOUND: bells rising together, then sudden silence)

A clanking deep below, as of a heavy chain dragged over casks. The cellar-door booms. The noise comes up the stairs, straight toward his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without pause the shape passes through the heavy door. The dying flame leaps up.

MARLEY'S GHOST stands before him: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots, transparent so coat-buttons show through; chain clasped about his middle, made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, heavy steel purses. Kerchief bound about head and chin.

(SOUND: chain drag and clank, flame whoosh, death-cold hush)

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits opposite the fireplace. Hair, skirts, and tassels still agitated as by hot vapour.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY'S GHOST
I don't.

SCROOGE
A slight disorder of the stomach makes them cheats. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. Scrooge clings to his chair. The Ghost removes the bandage; its lower jaw drops upon its breast.

(SOUND: appalling chain rattle, jaw drop click)

Scrooge falls on his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will. Is its pattern strange to you? Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house—mark me!—in life. Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

It flings the chain heavily upon the ground.

(SOUND: chain crash on floorboards)

MARLEY'S GHOST
At this time of the rolling year I suffer most. Hear me! My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds the wrapper round its head—teeth smart together—walks backward. The window rises by itself. Confused wailings fill the air. Marley's Ghost joins the dirge and floats out into the bleak night.

(SOUND: window rattle up, lamentation on the wind, chain fading)

Scrooge at the window: phantoms in chains wandering, moaning; one old ghost with an iron safe at its ankle cannot help a woman with an infant on a door-step. They fade into mist. Scrooge closes the window, examines the still double-locked door, tries to say "Humbug," stops, goes to bed fully dressed, and falls asleep at once.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Dark. Church chimes strike the quarters, then twelve. Scrooge's repeater beats twelve. He gropes to the frosted window—still night, still fog. He lies awake. Quarters pass.

SCROOGE
Ding, dong! A quarter past. Half-past! A quarter to it. The hour itself—and nothing else!

The deep, dull, hollow, melancholy ONE. Light flashes. Bed-curtains drawn aside by a hand.

(SOUND: heavy ONE, light whoosh, curtain rings)

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST: childlike yet old, white hair, unwrinkled face with tender bloom, long muscular arms, bare legs and feet, white tunic, lustrous belt, holly branch, summer flowers on dress; bright clear jet of light from its head; great extinguisher-cap under its arm. The figure fluctuates—now one arm, now twenty legs—then clear again.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
(what business)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare! Your reclamation, then. Take heed! Rise! and walk with me!

It clasps his arm. They move toward the window. Scrooge resists.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit touches his heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Clear cold winter day, snow on the ground. Fields either hand. City gone. Scrooge, still in nightclothes, clasps his hands.

(SOUND: crisp snow underfoot, distant boys shouting Merry Christmas)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
(a pimple—lead on)

They walk. Shaggy ponies, boys in gigs, merry music in the air.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOL CLASSROOM - DAY

Long bare melancholy room, deal forms and desks. A lonely BOY SCROOGE reads near a feeble fire. Adult Scrooge sits and weeps.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from water-spout, empty door swing)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

Imagination fills the window: Ali Baba, Valentine and Orson, the Parrot, Friday. Scrooge laughs and cries.

SCROOGE
Poor boy! I wish—but it's too late now. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost smiles, waves its hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

The boy grows larger. Room darker, dirtier. He paces despairingly. The door opens. Little FAN, much younger, darts in, arms about his neck.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

BOY SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

They go. A schoolmaster's terrible voice in the hall. They drive off in a chaise through hoar-frost.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. God forbid I should gainsay it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Old FEZZIWIG in a Welsh wig at a high desk. Clock strikes seven. He rubs his hands, laughs all over himself.

(SOUND: clock chime, fiddle tuning like fifty stomach-aches)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

YOUNG SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS charge the shutters—up, barred, pinned—back panting. Floor swept, lamps trimmed, fire heaped. In come Mrs. Fezziwig, three Miss Fezziwigs, followers, employees, housemaid, baker, cook, milkman—twenty couple dancing. Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig lead Sir Roger de Coverley; his calves shine like moons; he cuts so deftly he seems to wink with his legs.

(MUSIC: lively fiddle, Sir Roger de Coverley)

At eleven, Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig shake every hand at the door: Merry Christmas. The lads to beds under the counter.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. CITY STREET / INT. BELLE'S ROOM - EVENING

Young Scrooge, prime of life, face beginning to show care and avarice, sits with BELLE in mourning-dress; tears in her eyes.

(SOUND: soft fire crackle, distant street)

BELLE
It matters little. Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

YOUNG SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. If you were free to-day, can even I believe that you would choose a dowerless girl—you who weigh everything by Gain? I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. They part.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

Another room: Belle now a comely matron, husband, tumble of children, Christmas joy. Husband mentions he saw Mr. Scrooge alone in his office—partner dying—quite alone in the world.

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it! Leave me! Take me back. Haunt me no longer!

He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down. Light still streams from under it. Exhausted, he reels to his own bed and sinks into heavy sleep.

(SOUND: struggle, light hiss under cap, bed creak)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes in a tough snore. Bell upon the stroke of One. He parts all curtains himself. A blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room. He shuffles to the door. A strange voice calls his name.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

Transformed: living green on walls and ceiling, holly, mistletoe, ivy; mighty blaze in the hearth; throne of turkeys, geese, game, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, oysters, chestnuts, apples, oranges, seething punch. Upon it sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—jolly Giant in green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast and feet, holly wreath with icicles, glowing torch like Plenty's horn, antique empty scabbard eaten with rust.

(SOUND: fire roar, punch seethe, torch hiss)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family? More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for. Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge holds fast. Room, food, fire vanish.

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Snow on roofs, people scraping pavements, boys pelting snow from housetops, cheerful despite gloom. Poulterers and fruiterers radiant. Church bells. Poor people carry dinners to bakers. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch; angry jostlers recover good humour.

(SOUND: snow scrape, bells, market bustle, torch sprinkle)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

They pass on to the suburbs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, poorly dressed but brave in ribbons, lays the cloth with BELINDA. PETER plunges a fork into potatoes, monstrous shirt-collar choking him. Two young Cratchits scream of the goose. MARTHA arrives late from the milliner's; hides; BOB CRATCHIT enters with at least three feet of comforter and TINY TIM upon his shoulder—little crutch, iron frame on limbs.

(SOUND: kettle, children’s shrieks, crutch tip on floor)

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha runs from the closet into his arms. The young Cratchits bear Tiny Tim off to hear the pudding sing in the copper.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles. Tiny Tim's crutch returns; he to his stool. Goose procession. Grace. Mrs. Cratchit plunges the knife; stuffing gushes; Tiny Tim beats the table and cries Hurrah. Wonderful goose. Then the pudding—speckled cannon-ball, blazing brandy, holly on top.

(SOUND: knife in goose, delighted murmur, brandy whoosh, chestnuts crack)

Cloth cleared. Hot gin-and-lemon in two tumblers and a custard-cup without handle. Bob proposes:

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL CRATCHITS
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim close at Bob's side; Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head at his own words.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. Odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man. I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The shadow of his name passes. They grow merrier. Tiny Tim sings. The Spirit's torch brightens them as they fade. Scrooge's eye stays on Tiny Tim to the last.

EXT. BLEAK MOOR / LIGHTHOUSE / SHIP - NIGHT

Montage under the Spirit's robe: miners' hut, old song and chorus; solitary lighthouse, two men with grog wishing Merry Christmas; ship at sea, every man a Christmas thought.

(SOUND: wind on moor, sea thunder, rough chorus, waves)

INT. FRED'S DRAWING-ROOM - NIGHT

Bright dry gleaming room. FRED laughing holding his sides. NIECE (pretty, dimpled) and friends roar with him. Topper eyes the plump sister.

(SOUND: hearty laughter, harp, then blind-man's-buff thumps)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, and not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him. I mean to give him the same chance every year. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it if he finds me going there, year after year. If it only puts him in the vein to leave his poor clerk fifty pounds, that's something.

Games: blind-man's buff (Topper always finds the plump sister), forfeits, Yes and No. They guess Fred's animal—Uncle Scrooge. They drink his health.

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge, unseen, would have pledged them. The scene passes.

EXT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

The Ghost has grown old; hair grey. Chimes ring three quarters past eleven.

(SOUND: chimes, cold wind)

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night. To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the robe: two children, wretched, yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish—boy and girl—cling to its garment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost is gone. A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, comes like a mist along the ground.

(SOUND: twelve strokes, then dead silence)

EXT. CITY / 'CHANGE - NIGHT

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME: deep black garment, nothing visible but one outstretched hand. Scrooge kneels, legs trembling.

(SOUND: distant money chink, footsteps, no voice from the Spirit)

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The garment contracts as if the Spirit nods. The hand points onward.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company. Lead on! Lead on!

Merchants on 'Change. A fat man with monstrous chin:

FAT MAN
I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead. Last night, I believe. What has he done with his money? Left it to his Company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. Upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

Laughter. Another pair:

MR. ADAMS
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. BARKER
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Good morning!

Scrooge looks for himself—another man in his corner. The Phantom glides on.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT

Obscure foul street. Low shop: iron, rags, bottles, bones. OLD JOE by a charcoal stove. CHARWOMAN, MRS. DILBER the laundress, and UNDERTAKER'S MAN enter with bundles, burst out laughing.

(SOUND: rusty hinge shriek, pipe puff, coins on ground)

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

They open plunder: seal, pencil-case, sleeve-buttons; sheets, towels, teaspoons; then bed-curtains, blankets, and the dead man's best shirt taken off the body.

CHARWOMAN
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Bare uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a body plundered, unwatched, unwept. Pale light on it. Cat at the door; rats beneath the hearth.

(SOUND: cat scratch, rats gnaw, awful stillness)

The Spirit points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the cover.

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. Let us go! If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' HOME - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, anxious; children. Husband enters, careworn yet with shameful serious delight.

(SOUND: children’s hush, clock)

HUSBAND
He is past relenting. He is dead.

She is thankful in her soul, then prays forgiveness. Their hearts are lighter. The only emotion: pleasure at the death.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and girls sewing. Peter reads. The noisy Cratchits still as statues.

(SOUND: soft fire, needle, then Bob’s broken sob)

PETER
(reading)
And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes. Ah, poor Tiny Tim! It must be near his time.

PETER
Past it rather. He has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble.

Bob enters in his comforter. They help his tea. He is cheerful, then breaks:

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs to the child laid out, kisses the little face, comes down happy. He tells of Fred's kindness in the street. They will never forget poor Tiny Tim.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

Spirit of Tiny Tim, childish essence from God. Scrooge turns to the Phantom.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Iron gate. Walled churchyard, grass and weeds, choked with burying. The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to one.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, far bell)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only? Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit immovable, points. Scrooge creeps, reads upon the neglected grave:

EBENEZER SCROOGE

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

The hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the hand. The Spirit repulses him. Holding up his hands in a last prayer, he sees the hood and dress shrink, collapse, dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed, the room, his own. Time before him his own.

(SOUND: church bells pealing lustily—clash, clang, hammer, ding, dong)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He scrambles out, laughing and crying, tangled in stockings.

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. I am as giddy as a drunken man. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He frisks into the sitting-room—gruel saucepan, Marley's door, Present's corner, the window—all real.

SCROOGE
It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

He runs to the window, opens it. No fog: clear, bright, jovial cold; golden sunlight; merry bells.

SCROOGE
What's to-day!

A BOY in Sunday clothes below.

BOY
Eh?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?—Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He writes the address with shaking hand, pats the knocker.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - DAY

Scrooge dressed in his best, walks with hands behind him, delighted smile. Passers-by: Good morning, sir! A merry Christmas to you! He finds the portly Charity Gentleman.

(SOUND: street bustle, bright greetings, footsteps light)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you come and see me?

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

He goes to church, pats children, questions beggars, finds pleasure in everything. Afternoon: he turns toward Fred's house, passes the door a dozen times, then knocks.

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - DAY

Table spread in great array. Scrooge sidles his face round the door.

(SOUND: fire, cutlery, sudden joyous stir)

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off. Hearty welcome. Niece, Topper, plump sister, wonderful party, wonderful unanimity, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge early, door wide open to the Tank. Clock strikes nine. No Bob. A quarter past. Bob, full eighteen minutes and a half behind, hat and comforter off, on his stool driving his pen.

(SOUND: clock strike, pen scratch, stool scrape)

SCROOGE
(growling, feigning)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool, digs Bob in the waistcoat so Bob staggers back into the Tank.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob trembles, reaches toward the ruler—then understands. Joy breaks over him.

EXT. LONDON / CRATCHIT DOOR - DAY

NARRATOR over images: Scrooge better than his word; to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, a second father; as good a friend, master, and man as the good old city knew. Some laugh at the alteration; he lets them. His own heart laughs. No further intercourse with Spirits. It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well.

(MUSIC: warm swelling carol theme)

(SOUND: Tim’s crutch, then laughter, then soft bells)

TINY TIM (V.O.)
God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
